1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,440
Where secret homes and hideaways meet survival of the fittest,

2
00:00:04,441 --> 00:00:07,600
animal builders that claw their way to the top

3
00:00:07,601 --> 00:00:10,040
and sink their teeth into their work,

4
00:00:10,041 --> 00:00:14,040
all to make a little place they can call home.

5
00:00:14,041 --> 00:00:17,560
Now, Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,560
Mighty dams that hold back the wildest of rivers,

7
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:36,560
intricate tunnels that connect entire communities,

8
00:00:36,561 --> 00:00:40,560
and towering skyscrapers buzzing with life.

9
00:00:40,561 --> 00:00:44,560
This is construction animal style, where every day the world

10
00:00:44,561 --> 00:00:48,300
by creatures seemingly designed to alter their habitat.

11
00:00:48,301 --> 00:00:50,800
We call them nature's engineers.

12
00:00:50,801 --> 00:00:54,200
The way most animals that build things do it

13
00:00:54,201 --> 00:00:56,700
is by running through a series of routines.

14
00:00:56,701 --> 00:00:59,600
What they do is the same thing over and over and over again

15
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:03,720
until they reach some criterion, which they recognize innately.

16
00:01:03,721 --> 00:01:05,420
Then they switch to the next routine,

17
00:01:05,421 --> 00:01:07,620
and they run through it over and over again.

18
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:14,560
Dam building is one of humanity's earliest triumphs.

19
00:01:14,561 --> 00:01:17,760
Our ancestors' ability to create reservoirs that held precious

20
00:01:17,761 --> 00:01:22,160
water was paramount for early agriculture.

21
00:01:22,161 --> 00:01:25,800
Later, humans began to understand the power generating potential

22
00:01:25,801 --> 00:01:29,200
in holding back water and slowly releasing it to drive water

23
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:32,320
wheels. Over millennia, the art and science

24
00:01:32,321 --> 00:01:35,720
of dam building has been perfected with such massive

25
00:01:35,721 --> 00:01:38,720
water stopping and power generating structures as the

26
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:42,520
Hoover Dam. But long before the first human

27
00:01:42,521 --> 00:01:46,220
the first human industry tried to block even a tiny stream,

28
00:01:46,221 --> 00:01:49,220
an animal craftsman was fashioning dams of great size

29
00:01:49,221 --> 00:01:51,260
and sophistication.

30
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:54,960
One of my favorite animals is beaver.

31
00:01:54,961 --> 00:01:57,460
With the exception of corals, building huge coral reefs,

32
00:01:57,461 --> 00:02:00,480
they create the largest structures that are made by

33
00:02:00,481 --> 00:02:04,080
animals, sometimes a quarter mile across, sufficiently

34
00:02:04,081 --> 00:02:06,620
well made that you can ride a horse across the dam,

35
00:02:06,621 --> 00:02:08,380
for example.

36
00:02:08,381 --> 00:02:10,380
Like most of nature's engineers,

37
00:02:10,381 --> 00:02:12,980
The beaver's purpose is to construct a secure home

38
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:17,220
to raise its young and stay safe from predators.

39
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:18,880
For a pair of mating beavers, this

40
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:22,320
means building a sturdy home called a lodge.

41
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:24,520
But the animals are very particular when it comes to

42
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:25,920
location.

43
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:28,640
For added protection, they prefer their home be surrounded

44
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,340
by water.

45
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,940
For many, the first step in building a house

46
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,340
is building a lake.

47
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:36,880
And that involves creating one of nature's most sizable

48
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,380
engineering feats, the beaver dam.

49
00:02:40,381 --> 00:02:47,380
But even the largest structures must begin with the felling of a single tree.

50
00:02:47,381 --> 00:02:55,380
Down here near the water we have a prime example of beaver activity.

51
00:02:55,381 --> 00:03:02,380
You see down on the base, the beaver's gotten in here, chiseled away with those chisel-type

52
00:03:02,381 --> 00:03:06,380
teeth, gotten into the meat of the tree or the cambion layer.

53
00:03:06,381 --> 00:03:08,380
If a beaver takes down this willow,

54
00:03:08,381 --> 00:03:11,380
its likely uses would be for dam building or for forage.

55
00:03:11,381 --> 00:03:14,380
And if the beaver's gonna forage off the tree,

56
00:03:14,381 --> 00:03:18,380
it would likely use some of the limbs up top

57
00:03:18,381 --> 00:03:21,380
that are more easily digestible, the bark's thinner.

58
00:03:21,381 --> 00:03:23,380
If the beaver uses it for dam building,

59
00:03:23,381 --> 00:03:24,380
once the tree's down,

60
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:27,380
it's probably gonna strip off some of the branches.

61
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,380
For humans, even with the proper tools,

62
00:03:33,381 --> 00:03:36,220
felling a tree is hard work.

63
00:03:36,221 --> 00:03:38,680
But beavers are well adapted for this task,

64
00:03:38,681 --> 00:03:42,120
and the only tools they need are right in their own skulls.

65
00:03:42,121 --> 00:03:44,220
Here's a beaver skull, and you can see

66
00:03:44,221 --> 00:03:47,220
that the front two teeth are very sharp.

67
00:03:47,221 --> 00:03:50,140
There's a layer of hard enamel on the outside

68
00:03:50,141 --> 00:03:52,740
and soft dentine, and they work together.

69
00:03:52,741 --> 00:03:55,240
And when they touch, they sharpen each other.

70
00:03:55,241 --> 00:03:58,940
This tooth extends almost this far into the lower jaw,

71
00:03:58,941 --> 00:04:00,440
likewise in the upper jaw.

72
00:04:00,441 --> 00:04:04,680
They have extremely powerful muscles that operate those jaws.

73
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:06,840
Those masseter muscles that attach here

74
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:10,680
to bite through that wood are very, very strong.

75
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:13,380
The rest of the beaver's body is equally well designed

76
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:15,380
for aquatic construction.

77
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:18,900
Their small, agile front feet let them weave branches

78
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,100
and scoop mud, two very important steps in dam building.

79
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,400
In contrast to the popularly held cartoon image,

80
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:31,800
Beavers do not use their broad, flat tails to shovel and pack mud.

81
00:04:31,801 --> 00:04:34,800
Instead, they use them as rudders when swimming.

82
00:04:34,801 --> 00:04:42,240
And if the animal is startled, they slap their tails against the water to sound an alarm.

83
00:04:42,241 --> 00:04:47,160
The beaver's webbed rear feet also allow them to move effortlessly through the water,

84
00:04:47,161 --> 00:04:52,160
which is important, because if you're going to build a dam, you have to get your feet wet.

85
00:04:52,161 --> 00:04:56,160
The beavers will start chewing off small twigs and branches off of trees

86
00:04:56,161 --> 00:04:58,960
and plugging them into areas where they can get a foothold

87
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:01,860
in the bottom of the stream so that as they pack mud

88
00:05:01,861 --> 00:05:04,160
and more sticks and bigger sticks behind them,

89
00:05:04,161 --> 00:05:07,000
the water starts to build up.

90
00:05:07,001 --> 00:05:09,200
Where rising water behind an unfinished dam

91
00:05:09,201 --> 00:05:12,000
would spell disaster to human engineers,

92
00:05:12,001 --> 00:05:13,920
beavers seem to recognize and exploit

93
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:16,120
the construction advantages.

94
00:05:16,121 --> 00:05:18,920
Beaver are built to deal with water,

95
00:05:18,921 --> 00:05:20,820
and when they are building their dam,

96
00:05:20,821 --> 00:05:23,520
they take use of the fact that wood floats.

97
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:26,460
They harvest stuff around the dam.

98
00:05:26,461 --> 00:05:29,160
If they have to, they will build channels way out,

99
00:05:29,161 --> 00:05:32,160
long ways out, deep channels, 18 inches deep,

100
00:05:32,161 --> 00:05:33,760
that will fill with water.

101
00:05:33,761 --> 00:05:34,960
They'll drop the trees.

102
00:05:34,961 --> 00:05:36,260
They'll pull them in.

103
00:05:36,261 --> 00:05:39,660
It's like a barge dragging the stuff using the water.

104
00:05:39,661 --> 00:05:43,580
So a lot of the material comes from upstream.

105
00:05:43,581 --> 00:05:45,280
The final step for most beavers

106
00:05:45,281 --> 00:05:49,080
is the packing of stones and mud between the loose wood.

107
00:05:49,081 --> 00:05:52,180
The exterior coating helps to solidify the structure

108
00:05:52,181 --> 00:05:55,540
and keeps water from seeping through small cracks and fissures.

109
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:58,740
But the beaver's job is never really finished.

110
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,880
The animal will continue to maintain the structure from season to season.

111
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:09,600
Beaver don't have any need to keep their dams as absolute zero-flow devices.

112
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:16,260
Although during much of the year they'll busily repair any damage, find any leaks, and patch them.

113
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,780
In other parts of the year, when there's a lot of stream flow,

114
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:22,420
They may actually lower the dam to provide a spillway

115
00:06:22,421 --> 00:06:25,380
to take strain off of the dam.

116
00:06:25,381 --> 00:06:28,560
And during the winter, they'll wait until the ice is formed,

117
00:06:28,561 --> 00:06:31,320
and then they'll go and actually cut a hole just below the ice

118
00:06:31,321 --> 00:06:34,220
through the dam and lower the water level slightly

119
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:37,260
so that they have an airspace to breathe in between the water

120
00:06:37,261 --> 00:06:38,000
and the ice.

121
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:43,900
But for all its fame, the Beaver Dam

122
00:06:43,901 --> 00:06:46,340
is only a means to an end.

123
00:06:46,341 --> 00:06:49,040
The Beaver Dam is the most obvious structure

124
00:06:49,041 --> 00:06:51,680
in the beaver's habitat,

125
00:06:51,681 --> 00:06:53,440
but the pond behind the beaver dam

126
00:06:53,441 --> 00:06:56,120
is really where the beaver lives and where it thrives.

127
00:06:56,121 --> 00:06:57,720
The lodge is built there.

128
00:06:57,721 --> 00:07:00,220
That provides the animal protection from predators

129
00:07:00,221 --> 00:07:01,460
and from the elements.

130
00:07:01,461 --> 00:07:03,920
These lodges are really fascinating.

131
00:07:03,921 --> 00:07:07,300
When I was growing up, I thought that when a beaver made a lodge,

132
00:07:07,301 --> 00:07:10,620
it actually built a hole in there as it was building up,

133
00:07:10,621 --> 00:07:12,200
and when it completed the structure,

134
00:07:12,201 --> 00:07:13,940
it would have the hole in there.

135
00:07:13,941 --> 00:07:15,140
But they don't.

136
00:07:15,141 --> 00:07:20,220
pack stuff, logs and branches and mud.

137
00:07:20,221 --> 00:07:24,580
And then when they're done, they go from outside underneath

138
00:07:24,581 --> 00:07:27,940
with their claws and with their teeth,

139
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:31,640
and they dig away at the packed structure that they've made

140
00:07:31,641 --> 00:07:33,420
and hollow out their lodge.

141
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,280
The inside of the lodge isn't all that big.

142
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:39,840
You know, this one is probably 12 feet across

143
00:07:39,841 --> 00:07:41,620
and five or so feet tall,

144
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,420
but the inside is only a few feet across

145
00:07:44,421 --> 00:07:45,920
and a few feet tall.

146
00:07:45,921 --> 00:07:49,160
Most beaver lodges are two-story affairs.

147
00:07:49,161 --> 00:07:52,660
The first story, or chamber, is closest to the entrance

148
00:07:52,661 --> 00:07:56,800
and is used by the animal to dry off upon returning home.

149
00:07:56,801 --> 00:07:59,460
The second is where the animals spend most of their time huddled

150
00:07:59,461 --> 00:08:00,460
together for warmth.

151
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:06,700
Beavers take great care when constructing their homes

152
00:08:06,701 --> 00:08:10,420
because they often plan on staying a long time.

153
00:08:10,421 --> 00:08:13,720
The beaver will add to the lodge every year.

154
00:08:13,721 --> 00:08:18,320
As you can see, it was mainly a mud and small twig lodge,

155
00:08:18,321 --> 00:08:21,120
and it's dragged up some pretty big pieces.

156
00:08:21,121 --> 00:08:23,220
Later on, if it goes out further,

157
00:08:23,221 --> 00:08:24,760
or if the pond becomes deeper,

158
00:08:24,761 --> 00:08:26,900
and it has access to even larger trees,

159
00:08:26,901 --> 00:08:29,040
it will tote some of those across.

160
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:31,800
On some of the lakes around here that have beaver lodges,

161
00:08:31,801 --> 00:08:33,540
some of the logs that are brought up

162
00:08:33,541 --> 00:08:36,740
are six or so inches in diameter.

163
00:08:36,741 --> 00:08:39,240
In the end, here is where the beaver's months

164
00:08:39,241 --> 00:08:41,620
of hard work pays off.

165
00:08:41,621 --> 00:08:43,880
Protected from the elements and predators,

166
00:08:43,881 --> 00:08:46,800
the pair welcomes a new generation of baby builders

167
00:08:46,801 --> 00:08:48,660
to the lodge.

168
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:50,860
Beavers are incredible engineers.

169
00:08:50,861 --> 00:08:53,460
They may not really understand what they're building,

170
00:08:53,461 --> 00:08:57,060
but the results of what they create, the huge lodge,

171
00:08:57,061 --> 00:09:01,100
the large dams, have a profound effect on the ecosystem.

172
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:05,440
In this situation, they built a dam on the outlet of a lake,

173
00:09:05,441 --> 00:09:10,180
and they raised the water level of this lake almost three feet.

174
00:09:10,181 --> 00:09:17,680
A 40 or 50 acre lake, and that's 350 acre feet of water,

175
00:09:17,681 --> 00:09:19,680
which is an amazing amount of water

176
00:09:19,681 --> 00:09:25,220
to be backed up behind a pile of sticks and mud and rock.

177
00:09:25,221 --> 00:09:28,800
The dam's inelegance is the secret to its strength.

178
00:09:28,801 --> 00:09:32,760
But not all animals share the same crude design philosophy.

179
00:09:32,761 --> 00:09:36,140
Beavers may be some of the best known builders on the planet,

180
00:09:36,141 --> 00:09:38,800
but their wooden creations look simple compared

181
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:41,840
to the complex structures built by some of nature's most

182
00:09:41,841 --> 00:09:46,800
exacting engineers. The largest beaver dam on record

183
00:09:46,801 --> 00:09:51,800
was located in Three Forks, Montana, and measured over 2,100

184
00:09:51,801 --> 00:09:54,800
feet long. Nature's Engineers will return

185
00:09:54,801 --> 00:09:57,800
on Modern Marvels.

186
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:06,800
We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

187
00:10:06,801 --> 00:10:10,500
With six sides intersecting at 60-degree angles,

188
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:14,140
the hexagon is one of nature's strongest and most efficient

189
00:10:14,141 --> 00:10:14,841
shapes.

190
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:18,520
The hexagon is the most efficient structure

191
00:10:18,521 --> 00:10:19,700
that can be built.

192
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,400
You have the maximum volume in the cell

193
00:10:22,401 --> 00:10:24,900
with the minimum structure on the outside,

194
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:27,060
and each one fits perfectly to the next.

195
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:30,540
In the 1940s, the visionary Buckminster Fuller

196
00:10:30,541 --> 00:10:33,620
recognized the hexagon's potential in construction.

197
00:10:33,621 --> 00:10:36,920
The flower of his work was the space-age structure known as

198
00:10:36,921 --> 00:10:41,160
a geodesic dome, which offered stability and a lot of interior

199
00:10:41,161 --> 00:10:43,660
space.

200
00:10:43,661 --> 00:10:46,660
Hexagon-shaped materials are ideal whenever a structure must

201
00:10:46,661 --> 00:10:49,160
be light and strong.

202
00:10:49,161 --> 00:10:53,280
Today, they can be found just under the skin of jet fighters

203
00:10:53,281 --> 00:10:57,580
and even on the space shuttle.

204
00:10:57,581 --> 00:11:01,040
But long before the hexagon was protecting astronauts and orbiting

205
00:11:01,041 --> 00:11:02,900
and orbiting high above the Earth.

206
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:06,780
It could be found in a field and usually held something sweet.

207
00:11:07,740 --> 00:11:11,800
Honeybees build combs that have hexagonal cells in them.

208
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:14,720
This turns out to be one of the structurally strongest forms

209
00:11:14,721 --> 00:11:15,520
that they could do.

210
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:18,860
And it probably has more to do with actually

211
00:11:18,861 --> 00:11:22,120
how the cells fit together than intentionally making them

212
00:11:22,121 --> 00:11:22,880
to be strong.

213
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,900
But it works out that they can make very large structures

214
00:11:25,901 --> 00:11:27,140
that are very light in weight.

215
00:11:27,900 --> 00:11:29,960
Honeybees live throughout the world

216
00:11:29,961 --> 00:11:32,700
in all but the very coldest climates.

217
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:36,600
A buzzing, roiling hive is an animal metropolis held together

218
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:39,200
by careful engineering, communal living,

219
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:43,100
and some very sticky material.

220
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:44,600
Scientists who study these animals

221
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:48,120
take great care not to get on their subject's bad side.

222
00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:50,220
I'm attempting to light a smoker

223
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,020
because if you puff smoke into the colony,

224
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:54,420
you don't get met with as much resistance.

225
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:57,660
We have to use a hive tool to break in because the honeybees

226
00:11:57,661 --> 00:12:00,560
The honeybees collect plant resins, pine pitch,

227
00:12:00,561 --> 00:12:02,360
and they use that to seal every single crack.

228
00:12:02,361 --> 00:12:05,200
So every comb is stuck in, every box is stuck to the other box,

229
00:12:05,201 --> 00:12:07,700
the cover is stuck on, so we have to break in.

230
00:12:07,701 --> 00:12:10,040
Each hive is ruled by a queen.

231
00:12:10,041 --> 00:12:14,580
And when new queens are born, only one can stay.

232
00:12:14,581 --> 00:12:17,180
The honeycomb beehive takes shape when one of the queens,

233
00:12:17,181 --> 00:12:21,320
like this one, marked by the beekeeper with a yellow dot,

234
00:12:21,321 --> 00:12:24,320
and a portion of the worker bees leave a functioning hive

235
00:12:24,321 --> 00:12:25,820
to build a new home.

236
00:12:25,821 --> 00:12:28,560
The swarming insects look for knots in trees,

237
00:12:28,561 --> 00:12:32,060
clefts in rocks, or abandoned burrows in the ground.

238
00:12:32,061 --> 00:12:34,660
But on occasion, confused bees have been known to start

239
00:12:34,661 --> 00:12:38,360
building on top of cars.

240
00:12:38,361 --> 00:12:40,760
For many of these winged engineers,

241
00:12:40,761 --> 00:12:43,680
the best man-made structures are those built specifically

242
00:12:43,681 --> 00:12:46,140
for them.

243
00:12:46,141 --> 00:12:47,580
They already know that I'm here.

244
00:12:47,581 --> 00:12:49,720
And the reason that I can tell you that is they can feel

245
00:12:49,721 --> 00:12:50,580
vibrations.

246
00:12:50,581 --> 00:12:53,480
And so they are actually aware that something has come up.

247
00:12:53,481 --> 00:12:55,380
But these bees are pretty docile,

248
00:12:55,381 --> 00:12:57,220
so it's not that much of a problem.

249
00:12:57,221 --> 00:12:59,140
We put some smoke in the entrance.

250
00:12:59,141 --> 00:13:00,140
We can look around.

251
00:13:00,141 --> 00:13:01,840
If there's another place out here where there's a hole

252
00:13:01,841 --> 00:13:03,140
and they're using that as an entrance,

253
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:04,840
we'd have to smoke that too because that's where the guard

254
00:13:04,841 --> 00:13:06,980
bees are, but I don't see any.

255
00:13:06,981 --> 00:13:09,980
A mature functioning hive is a wonder of cooperation

256
00:13:09,981 --> 00:13:12,320
and organization.

257
00:13:12,321 --> 00:13:15,440
Over the course of their lives, each bee will perform nearly

258
00:13:15,441 --> 00:13:23,740
every task in the hive, including foraging, guard duty,

259
00:13:23,741 --> 00:13:26,740
babysitting. As young workers, the bees spend

260
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:31,240
time looking after the larvae developing in the brood comb.

261
00:13:31,241 --> 00:13:34,980
But the most important stage in a bee's life, for construction,

262
00:13:34,981 --> 00:13:37,980
is when they begin to produce their own unique and pliant

263
00:13:37,981 --> 00:13:44,400
building material, wax. The substance is formed by glands

264
00:13:44,401 --> 00:13:48,740
inside the bee's body. Once secreted, the wax collects

265
00:13:48,741 --> 00:13:53,740
in thin sheets between the plates of the insect's abdomen.

266
00:13:53,741 --> 00:13:57,340
Those glands usually get activated when the bee is fully

267
00:13:57,440 --> 00:13:58,740
engorged with nectar.

268
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:01,240
This occurs in two principal circumstances.

269
00:14:01,340 --> 00:14:03,380
One is if the colony is bringing in lots of nectar

270
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:06,880
from the outside, having a hard time finding a place to store

271
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:07,980
it.

272
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:09,980
Another circumstance is when a colony swarms,

273
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:12,800
the individuals in the swarm, before they leave the hive,

274
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:15,500
fill up their crops with honey.

275
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:17,800
The bees chew the newly manufactured wax

276
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:22,200
to make it malleable and seem to place it entirely by feel.

277
00:14:22,201 --> 00:14:25,900
How do the bees build these perfect hexagons?

278
00:14:25,901 --> 00:14:28,500
We don't know all of the answer yet,

279
00:14:28,501 --> 00:14:31,440
but one of the things we do know is that the size of them

280
00:14:31,441 --> 00:14:33,240
and presumably the angles between them

281
00:14:33,241 --> 00:14:35,940
are sensed by the bee, essentially with her forelegs.

282
00:14:35,941 --> 00:14:38,140
So if the legs of a bee are damaged,

283
00:14:38,141 --> 00:14:42,560
she will build an exact comb relative to a bee with intact legs.

284
00:14:42,561 --> 00:14:45,160
For scientists, other questions arise,

285
00:14:45,161 --> 00:14:49,960
such as how do all the bees know in which direction to build?

286
00:14:49,961 --> 00:14:55,480
Bees appear to use magnetic fields to help them line up those combs parallel to each other,

287
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,540
so that when a colony moves into a new cavity,

288
00:14:59,080 --> 00:15:03,780
the orientation of the combs that they tend to build has been shown to be correlated

289
00:15:03,781 --> 00:15:08,480
with the orientation of the combs in their previous hive, in their previous nest.

290
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:12,840
And if an experimenter experimentally removes the Earth's magnetic field

291
00:15:12,841 --> 00:15:17,140
by using electromagnetic coils, that consistency is lost.

292
00:15:17,141 --> 00:15:21,400
In the end, all the cells will be built almost exactly the same,

293
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:25,100
with between 25 and 30 cells per square inch.

294
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,240
The bees' construction is so uniform that beekeepers are able

295
00:15:30,241 --> 00:15:34,380
to install man-made foundation, special wax sheets with molded

296
00:15:34,381 --> 00:15:36,900
hexagons, into their box hives.

297
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:39,680
The bees build directly onto the foundation,

298
00:15:40,280 --> 00:15:42,640
and the result is honeycomb in a box.

299
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:47,120
The length of the cells vary depending on species.

300
00:15:47,140 --> 00:15:49,740
in intended use.

301
00:15:49,741 --> 00:15:52,180
Honeycomb cells can be some of the longest,

302
00:15:52,181 --> 00:15:54,640
while broodcomb cells built to house the queen's eggs

303
00:15:54,641 --> 00:15:57,040
are shorter, usually just big enough

304
00:15:57,041 --> 00:15:59,780
to house the growing larvae.

305
00:15:59,781 --> 00:16:01,380
The cells are adjusted seasonally

306
00:16:01,381 --> 00:16:05,000
depending on the kind of individuals they want to raise.

307
00:16:05,001 --> 00:16:09,300
Queens require a larger cell, so when they want to turn a worker

308
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:12,240
larva into a queen, they actually lengthen the cell

309
00:16:12,241 --> 00:16:15,540
by half again probably and give it much more food

310
00:16:15,541 --> 00:16:18,240
and special nutrients.

311
00:16:18,340 --> 00:16:20,380
The queen bee seems to inspect each cell

312
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:23,020
in the brood comb before depositing one of the over half

313
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:27,220
a million eggs she will lay during her lifetime.

314
00:16:27,320 --> 00:16:30,020
If any little heads come up, use a little bit more smoke.

315
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:33,960
OK, in this particular comb, we have mostly capped brood,

316
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:37,260
which is the suede-looking material that's

317
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:38,800
a little reddish in color.

318
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:41,540
And then out here by my right thumb, this is honey.

319
00:16:41,640 --> 00:16:44,340
You can see that the cappings are a little bit shinier.

320
00:16:44,341 --> 00:16:48,340
There's a very small amount of pollen right along here,

321
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:50,340
and also along here.

322
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:54,340
So this is the classic honey over the top, pollen

323
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:57,180
along the edges, brewed in the middle comb,

324
00:16:57,280 --> 00:17:01,100
like you would see a picture of in a textbook somewhere.

325
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:02,900
But survival of the species demands

326
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,300
that bees, even in the perfect home,

327
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:07,700
must eventually leave.

328
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:11,240
From old hives come new swarms, which fly off

329
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:13,900
to make a new home and start the entire geometric

330
00:17:13,901 --> 00:17:15,900
construction process again.

331
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:23,240
But bees aren't the only insect city builders

332
00:17:23,340 --> 00:17:25,340
to make use of the hexagon.

333
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:27,840
Wasps have a similar construction style

334
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:30,440
to house their offspring.

335
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:33,180
Starting with a strong branch, the wasps build

336
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:36,420
a bell-shaped cone with each cell large enough

337
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:38,060
to fit a wasp larva.

338
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:40,220
Paper wasps make their colonies,

339
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:43,000
and they actually do have hexagonal cells in those,

340
00:17:43,001 --> 00:17:44,800
just like the honeybees do.

341
00:17:44,801 --> 00:17:46,740
But these insects don't produce wax,

342
00:17:46,741 --> 00:17:49,540
and so they construct their colonies out of plant fibers

343
00:17:49,541 --> 00:17:52,940
that they collect by scraping fibers off leaves.

344
00:17:52,941 --> 00:17:55,440
They mix that with saliva, and then they build the paper

345
00:17:55,441 --> 00:17:56,940
using that material.

346
00:17:56,941 --> 00:17:58,740
So it's a different kind of construction,

347
00:17:58,741 --> 00:18:01,960
but sort of similar engineering principles.

348
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:04,760
Just as with the honeybees' creation,

349
00:18:04,761 --> 00:18:07,660
the hexagonal shape allows all the cells to fit together

350
00:18:07,661 --> 00:18:09,860
in a modular fashion.

351
00:18:09,861 --> 00:18:11,860
Once finished with a group of cells,

352
00:18:11,861 --> 00:18:14,400
many wasps construct additional combs

353
00:18:14,401 --> 00:18:17,400
and stack them like floors in a hotel.

354
00:18:17,401 --> 00:18:20,400
That material is supplied in a series of layers

355
00:18:20,401 --> 00:18:22,000
with air spaces between it.

356
00:18:22,001 --> 00:18:25,100
It provides good insulation to help keep the colonies warm.

357
00:18:25,101 --> 00:18:28,200
Wasp colonies are sometimes built out in the open

358
00:18:28,201 --> 00:18:30,820
as sort of football-shaped nests

359
00:18:30,821 --> 00:18:32,620
that you might see hanging in trees.

360
00:18:32,621 --> 00:18:36,620
Other times they're built inside of cavities in the ground.

361
00:18:36,621 --> 00:18:38,920
But no matter where wasps build,

362
00:18:38,921 --> 00:18:41,860
their survival depends on the strength of their homes.

363
00:18:41,861 --> 00:18:45,360
Fortunately, their nests are unexpectedly tough.

364
00:18:45,361 --> 00:18:49,100
Actually, the paper they make is pretty waterproof.

365
00:18:49,101 --> 00:18:52,100
It's something in the saliva that they mix with the plant

366
00:18:52,101 --> 00:18:56,100
fibers makes it quite strong, very lightweight.

367
00:18:56,101 --> 00:18:58,480
But typically, the nest will only be used

368
00:18:58,481 --> 00:19:00,480
for one season.

369
00:19:00,481 --> 00:19:03,940
The wasps, like all animals, are practical engineers.

370
00:19:03,941 --> 00:19:06,560
Overbuilding a structure means extra work,

371
00:19:06,561 --> 00:19:09,980
and in the animal world, that equates to extra food,

372
00:19:09,981 --> 00:19:13,480
a luxury that's not always available.

373
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:17,180
But on rare occasions, when all the animal's needs are met,

374
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:20,060
the nests can take on a life of their own.

375
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:22,520
The largest wasp nest I've ever seen

376
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:24,820
was one that was excavated in Hawaii

377
00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:27,660
from the Western Yellow Jacket, which is a pest in Hawaii.

378
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:31,840
This nest had become perennial, and it was six feet by three

379
00:19:31,940 --> 00:19:34,180
feet, and it was in the ground.

380
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:36,600
And it consisted of probably hundreds of thousands

381
00:19:36,700 --> 00:19:38,100
of cells and workers.

382
00:19:38,101 --> 00:19:40,600
It was just enormous.

383
00:19:40,601 --> 00:19:42,900
I've never seen anything bigger.

384
00:19:42,901 --> 00:19:44,900
But winged insects aren't the only

385
00:19:44,901 --> 00:19:47,900
airborne animal engineers.

386
00:19:47,901 --> 00:19:51,400
Birds have been building nests for millions of years.

387
00:19:51,401 --> 00:19:53,900
They, too, are hardwired to build.

388
00:19:53,901 --> 00:19:57,900
That doesn't mean there's no room for improvement.

389
00:19:57,901 --> 00:20:01,900
During a good year, the U.S. honey industry can produce

390
00:20:01,901 --> 00:20:04,400
more than 215 million pounds of honey,

391
00:20:04,401 --> 00:20:08,640
of honey thanks to the work of well over 125 billion very

392
00:20:08,641 --> 00:20:09,640
busy bees.

393
00:20:09,641 --> 00:20:12,400
Nature's Engineers will return on Modern Marvels.

394
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:22,160
We now return to Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

395
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:24,760
There's no place like home.

396
00:20:24,761 --> 00:20:27,400
Humans have been subscribing to that philosophy since we began

397
00:20:27,401 --> 00:20:29,560
dwelling in caves.

398
00:20:29,561 --> 00:20:32,160
Today, houses are some of the most prolific structures

399
00:20:32,161 --> 00:20:34,200
in the face of the planet.

400
00:20:34,201 --> 00:20:37,160
But housing tracts filled with identical structures

401
00:20:37,161 --> 00:20:40,160
and one-of-a-kind mansions are simply the latest examples

402
00:20:40,161 --> 00:20:43,160
in a long line of structural evolution,

403
00:20:43,161 --> 00:20:46,160
an evolution that started thousands of years ago

404
00:20:46,161 --> 00:20:50,160
when people first gathered sticks, straw, rocks, and mud

405
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:54,160
to build their homes, something birds figured out

406
00:20:54,161 --> 00:20:57,160
how to do millions of years earlier.

407
00:20:57,161 --> 00:20:59,160
Why do birds build nests?

408
00:20:59,161 --> 00:21:02,200
Birds build nests to protect their young,

409
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:04,600
to shield them from adverse weather,

410
00:21:04,700 --> 00:21:06,660
to protect them from predators,

411
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:09,300
to make sure that the young have all of the warmth

412
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:11,300
that they need to develop correctly.

413
00:21:11,400 --> 00:21:15,100
Since all birds lay eggs, they don't give birth to live young.

414
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:17,620
They need to have protection for the eggs.

415
00:21:17,720 --> 00:21:19,420
All birds may lay eggs,

416
00:21:19,520 --> 00:21:22,420
but the nests they build vary greatly.

417
00:21:22,520 --> 00:21:25,120
Ultimately, time and evolution have had the greatest effect

418
00:21:25,220 --> 00:21:27,720
on the structures birds build.

419
00:21:27,721 --> 00:21:30,560
The earlier birds, the ones that you see records of being

420
00:21:30,660 --> 00:21:32,420
in the fossil record from way back,

421
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:35,620
those birds tend to build pretty simple nests constructed,

422
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:37,460
say, in the sand.

423
00:21:37,560 --> 00:21:40,160
The birds that have come on the scene more recently,

424
00:21:40,260 --> 00:21:42,760
those birds are the ones that build more complex nests

425
00:21:42,860 --> 00:21:45,380
and actually weave nests.

426
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:47,680
North American bald eagles build their homes

427
00:21:47,780 --> 00:21:50,880
from materials found on the forest floor.

428
00:21:50,980 --> 00:21:54,180
High in the treetops, eagles intertwine fallen branches

429
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:57,320
to form nests up to four feet wide.

430
00:21:57,321 --> 00:22:00,660
But no matter what their size, most stick-built nests

431
00:22:00,760 --> 00:22:02,760
share a similar characteristic.

432
00:22:02,860 --> 00:22:04,360
They're cozy inside.

433
00:22:04,460 --> 00:22:05,760
This is the nest of a house wren,

434
00:22:05,860 --> 00:22:07,860
which is common across North America.

435
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:10,760
And this is built generally inside some other structure.

436
00:22:10,860 --> 00:22:12,760
You can see the way the bird has constructed this nest.

437
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:15,480
It started out with larger fibers and branches

438
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:16,780
in the bottom part of the nest that

439
00:22:16,880 --> 00:22:19,480
allows the nest to withstand elements.

440
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:21,420
As you get closer and closer to the cup,

441
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:23,020
they start using more fine fibers.

442
00:22:23,120 --> 00:22:24,820
Here you can see small grasses.

443
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:27,080
And then the actual cup itself will be

444
00:22:27,081 --> 00:22:30,780
where the eggs are is lined with feathers.

445
00:22:30,781 --> 00:22:33,480
But not all birds are woodworkers.

446
00:22:33,481 --> 00:22:36,320
Many strive for something a little more concrete

447
00:22:36,321 --> 00:22:40,720
and make mud their construction material of choice.

448
00:22:40,721 --> 00:22:43,420
Swallows build their nests high on the walls of buildings

449
00:22:43,421 --> 00:22:46,600
or under bridges and freeway overpasses.

450
00:22:46,601 --> 00:22:50,040
Inside, the birds lay straw and feathers for insulation

451
00:22:50,041 --> 00:22:54,840
and to provide a soft floor for their eggs and young.

452
00:22:54,841 --> 00:22:58,740
Other birds don't need the strength of solid mud construction.

453
00:22:58,741 --> 00:23:03,140
Instead, they thrive by building small and inconspicuous nests.

454
00:23:03,141 --> 00:23:04,440
This is one of my favorite nests.

455
00:23:04,441 --> 00:23:06,480
This is the nest of an anise hummingbird,

456
00:23:06,481 --> 00:23:09,180
which is found along the Pacific coast of North America.

457
00:23:09,181 --> 00:23:11,280
And the anise hummingbird constructs its nest

458
00:23:11,281 --> 00:23:13,300
essentially using spider webs.

459
00:23:13,301 --> 00:23:16,400
And then it attaches lichens and other small grass

460
00:23:16,401 --> 00:23:18,300
and so forth around the edge of the nest,

461
00:23:18,301 --> 00:23:20,440
and then lines the nest with cottony material

462
00:23:20,441 --> 00:23:22,000
that it gets from plants.

463
00:23:22,001 --> 00:23:24,340
The lichen actually helps to conceal the nest

464
00:23:24,341 --> 00:23:28,340
on the branch, which is a camouflage that the bird relies

465
00:23:28,341 --> 00:23:31,140
on to keep other birds from finding its nest to predate

466
00:23:31,141 --> 00:23:32,140
its young.

467
00:23:32,141 --> 00:23:34,680
If you've ever tried to find hummingbird nests in a tree,

468
00:23:34,681 --> 00:23:36,780
it is extremely difficult.

469
00:23:36,781 --> 00:23:39,980
Masters of disguise, structural engineers,

470
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:41,700
homemakers.

471
00:23:41,701 --> 00:23:44,700
For humans, it's often easy to see creativity and intelligence

472
00:23:44,701 --> 00:23:46,400
in the bird world.

473
00:23:46,401 --> 00:23:48,100
It doesn't really exist.

474
00:23:48,101 --> 00:23:51,400
When it comes to birds, it's easy to believe that what we see

475
00:23:51,401 --> 00:23:53,300
involves more intelligence.

476
00:23:53,301 --> 00:23:58,040
And what they build looks superficially much more intelligent and much more complex.

477
00:23:58,320 --> 00:23:59,980
But in fact, it really isn't.

478
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:05,700
The idea that they have some concept of what the nest is going to look like is a romantic fiction.

479
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:07,640
It's been shown in lots of species of bird.

480
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:10,340
My favorite, though, are just birds that build simple cup nests.

481
00:24:10,341 --> 00:24:15,540
If you cut a hole in the bottom slightly larger than an egg, the birds will not repair it.

482
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:19,420
Instead, they will just mindlessly lay their eggs one a day into this nest.

483
00:24:19,421 --> 00:24:21,760
the eggs will roll out the bottom,

484
00:24:21,761 --> 00:24:24,860
and the birds will just keep right on laying them.

485
00:24:24,861 --> 00:24:28,160
Still, the birds don't rely entirely on hardwired routines

486
00:24:28,161 --> 00:24:30,360
when constructing nests.

487
00:24:30,361 --> 00:24:33,960
Their techniques prove that their brains are designed to learn.

488
00:24:33,961 --> 00:24:37,580
Birds definitely improve in their nest building capabilities

489
00:24:37,581 --> 00:24:39,080
from season to season.

490
00:24:39,081 --> 00:24:41,180
When they first start out, most bird species

491
00:24:41,181 --> 00:24:43,620
are not great at building nests.

492
00:24:43,621 --> 00:24:46,780
So as they practice, though, from season to season,

493
00:24:46,781 --> 00:24:49,540
They learn how to hold nest material.

494
00:24:49,541 --> 00:24:52,820
They learn how to pick particular plant materials

495
00:24:52,821 --> 00:24:56,260
that are better and will make a nest last longer.

496
00:24:56,261 --> 00:24:59,620
So usually by the second or third year of building a nest,

497
00:24:59,621 --> 00:25:01,500
the nests are made very, very well.

498
00:25:01,501 --> 00:25:03,300
I think we're fascinated with animals

499
00:25:03,301 --> 00:25:10,140
and the things they build, mainly because of the complexity

500
00:25:10,141 --> 00:25:13,340
and yet the relative ease with which the animals

501
00:25:13,341 --> 00:25:15,140
create these structures.

502
00:25:15,141 --> 00:25:18,840
You think about trying to build a nest yourself,

503
00:25:18,940 --> 00:25:21,780
you can realize that we would be very clumsy.

504
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:24,780
I used to have my animal behavior classes,

505
00:25:24,880 --> 00:25:27,580
sit around tables and try to make a robin's nest

506
00:25:27,680 --> 00:25:28,880
or an oriole nest.

507
00:25:28,980 --> 00:25:32,600
Very quickly, people threw in the towel.

508
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:34,500
And an oriole nest seems rudimentary

509
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:38,300
when compared to this, the ornithological equivalent

510
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:41,500
of the moonshot.

511
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,800
It's built by a type of African weaver

512
00:25:43,801 --> 00:25:46,600
