00:39:48,481 --> 00:39:52,520
So drag line soap, for example, can stretch about 25%

770
00:39:52,521 --> 00:39:54,420
beyond its original length.

771
00:39:54,421 --> 00:40:00,440
In contrast, some man-made fibers, such as high tensile steel

772
00:40:00,441 --> 00:40:04,840
or Kevlar, are very stiff, and they can't be stretched very far

773
00:40:04,841 --> 00:40:06,980
before they break.

774
00:40:06,981 --> 00:40:09,880
Well, this machine is a nano tensile tester

775
00:40:09,881 --> 00:40:12,480
tester that we use to look at the mechanical properties

776
00:40:12,481 --> 00:40:14,280
of spider silks.

777
00:40:14,281 --> 00:40:18,120
By mounting a sample of a single spider silk thread

778
00:40:18,121 --> 00:40:21,920
onto the machine, the machine can then gradually pull that

779
00:40:21,921 --> 00:40:25,620
thread and measure the amount of force that's needed to pull

780
00:40:25,621 --> 00:40:27,640
the thread until it breaks.

781
00:40:27,641 --> 00:40:31,240
This allows us to test how strong the fiber is,

782
00:40:31,241 --> 00:40:35,040
how stretchy the fiber is, and how tough it is.

783
00:40:35,041 --> 00:40:37,980
Spider silk may eventually lead to products stronger

784
00:40:37,981 --> 00:40:43,020
stronger than steel, but far lighter and much more flexible.

785
00:40:43,120 --> 00:40:44,860
With all its potential applications,

786
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:47,660
it's sometimes easy to forget that this material emanates

787
00:40:47,760 --> 00:40:50,720
from the end of a spider.

788
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:53,620
The animal physically pulls the final fibrous product

789
00:40:53,720 --> 00:40:57,000
from small spinnerets at the rear of its abdomen.

790
00:40:57,100 --> 00:40:58,760
But the silk production itself starts

791
00:40:58,860 --> 00:41:00,900
deep within the creature.

792
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,180
If you could look inside a spider's abdomen,

793
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:07,380
you would find anywhere from just a few to perhaps even

794
00:41:07,381 --> 00:41:11,380
thousands and thousands of tiny individual silk glands.

795
00:41:11,480 --> 00:41:14,880
From these silk glands, the silk travels down a narrow duct.

796
00:41:14,980 --> 00:41:17,020
And as it travels down the narrow duct,

797
00:41:17,120 --> 00:41:21,820
the liquid silk is transformed into a dry fiber.

798
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:24,300
So by the time the silk exits the spider

799
00:41:24,400 --> 00:41:26,340
through the spigots on the spinnerets,

800
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:28,740
we have a solid fiber.

801
00:41:28,840 --> 00:41:31,140
As impressive as the silk production is,

802
00:41:31,240 --> 00:41:34,980
what the spider does with the material is just as amazing.

803
00:41:35,080 --> 00:41:36,540
The best known spider constructions

804
00:41:36,541 --> 00:41:40,040
are the common circular webs, called orb webs.

805
00:41:40,041 --> 00:41:43,080
But their building process is anything but common.

806
00:41:43,081 --> 00:41:45,880
Now, an orb web is made up of several types

807
00:41:45,881 --> 00:41:47,480
of architectural elements.

808
00:41:47,481 --> 00:41:50,580
The first thing the spider does is construct the frame,

809
00:41:50,581 --> 00:41:52,480
and that's made from one type of silk.

810
00:41:52,481 --> 00:41:54,300
Then the next thing the spider does

811
00:41:54,301 --> 00:41:57,300
is lay down radii from a center point.

812
00:41:57,301 --> 00:42:00,700
So she lays out radii, and that's using the same kind of silk

813
00:42:00,701 --> 00:42:02,140
that was used in the frame.

814
00:42:02,141 --> 00:42:05,400
After the frame and radii are constructed,

815
00:42:05,401 --> 00:42:07,440
the spider prepares the capture spiral.

816
00:42:07,540 --> 00:42:10,600
And the capture spiral is a mixture of two types of silk.

817
00:42:10,700 --> 00:42:14,000
One is a stretchy fiber, and the other one

818
00:42:14,100 --> 00:42:16,440
is a gluey droplet that gets laid down

819
00:42:16,540 --> 00:42:18,240
over this stretchy piece of silk.

820
00:42:18,340 --> 00:42:21,320
And these gluey droplets allows insects to actually

821
00:42:21,420 --> 00:42:23,460
get stuck to the web.

822
00:42:23,560 --> 00:42:25,220
Every type of orb-weaving spider

823
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:27,520
spins a different web pattern.

824
00:42:27,620 --> 00:42:31,120
But the final product is always a lethal trap.

825
00:42:31,220 --> 00:42:33,660
The webs are able to snare insects many times bigger

826
00:42:33,661 --> 00:42:35,000
bigger than the spider.

827
00:42:35,001 --> 00:42:39,000
And on occasion, the unforgiving strands not only capture bats,

828
00:42:39,001 --> 00:42:41,300
but also small birds.

829
00:42:41,301 --> 00:42:44,100
Despite the silk's incredible toughness

830
00:42:44,101 --> 00:42:47,000
and the engineering involved in creating the webs,

831
00:42:47,001 --> 00:42:49,000
at the end of the day, the spiders

832
00:42:49,001 --> 00:42:52,220
often do something abhorrent to most engineers.

833
00:42:52,221 --> 00:42:54,520
They destroy their masterpieces

834
00:42:54,521 --> 00:42:57,220
to build them again the following day.

835
00:42:57,221 --> 00:42:59,960
The silk is largely made up of protein,

836
00:42:59,961 --> 00:43:02,960
and protein is a very valuable resource to an animal.

837
00:43:02,961 --> 00:43:08,200
So a typical orb-weaving spider will actually recycle their silk

838
00:43:08,201 --> 00:43:09,300
from day to day.

839
00:43:09,301 --> 00:43:12,000
If you ever watch a spider in your yard take a web down,

840
00:43:12,001 --> 00:43:15,000
she actually very carefully bundles all the silk up

841
00:43:15,001 --> 00:43:18,800
into a little ball, and then she'll actually consume that

842
00:43:18,801 --> 00:43:21,820
ball and recycle the amino acids from that web

843
00:43:21,821 --> 00:43:23,520
into tomorrow's web.

844
00:43:23,521 --> 00:43:26,660
The orb-weaver webs are some of the best known.

845
00:43:26,661 --> 00:43:29,060
They certainly aren't the only ones out there.

846
00:43:29,061 --> 00:43:31,920
Some spiders weave silken parachutes that help them

847
00:43:31,921 --> 00:43:33,360
take to the wind.

848
00:43:33,361 --> 00:43:39,320
Others build cozy subterranean homes,

849
00:43:39,321 --> 00:43:41,260
while some of the most infamous web spinners

850
00:43:41,261 --> 00:43:44,460
make what appears to be a mess.

851
00:43:44,461 --> 00:43:45,660
This is a black widow.

852
00:43:45,661 --> 00:43:47,760
The web that black widows make is very tough.

853
00:43:47,761 --> 00:43:50,980
And even though it's messy looking and not well engineered,

854
00:43:50,981 --> 00:43:53,480
it works perfectly for the kind of close quarters

855
00:43:53,481 --> 00:43:56,620
that black widows need their webs to be housed in.

856
00:43:56,621 --> 00:43:58,180
You can see how tough this is.

857
00:43:58,181 --> 00:44:01,180
I'm actually pulling the sticks inside with the web

858
00:44:01,181 --> 00:44:04,180
that she's got still attached.

859
00:44:04,181 --> 00:44:10,180
Spiders share a common thread with all animal engineers.

860
00:44:10,181 --> 00:44:14,180
These specialized creatures depend for their survival

861
00:44:14,181 --> 00:44:19,180
and their mysterious desire to manipulate the world around them.

862
00:44:19,181 --> 00:44:24,180
The fascination to me is that the animals create these incredibly complex

863
00:44:24,181 --> 00:44:29,180
and yet highly adapted structures without any prior experience.

864
00:44:29,181 --> 00:44:31,420
Just the idea that they know to make them,

865
00:44:31,520 --> 00:44:33,680
and they know how to make them, and that each species

866
00:44:33,780 --> 00:44:36,580
seems to make a different one is impressive compared

867
00:44:36,680 --> 00:44:39,520
to humans who seem to have no clue how to make anything

868
00:44:39,620 --> 00:44:40,920
without being trained.

869
00:44:41,020 --> 00:44:44,720
Our ability to learn and capacity for abstract thought

870
00:44:44,820 --> 00:44:47,040
may separate us from the animals.

871
00:44:47,140 --> 00:44:49,840
But no matter how massive our creations grow,

872
00:44:49,940 --> 00:44:53,280
or how advanced our construction materials become,

873
00:44:53,380 --> 00:44:55,680
animals around the world remind us

874
00:44:55,780 --> 00:44:58,940
that we aren't the first builders, but simply the latest

875
00:44:58,941 --> 00:45:02,940
in a long line of nature's engineers.

